meta {
  name: Get token
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{custodianUrl}}/token
  body: json
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "client_id": "{{clientId}}",
    "scope": "{{scope}}"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.body && res.body.access_token) {
    bru.setEnvVar("accessToken", res.body.access_token);
  }
}

docs {
  The full L2 flow in one call: the key custodian signs the private_key_jwt
  assertion AND runs the client_credentials exchange at the auth server, then
  returns the token response. The access token is saved to `accessToken` and
  carries this party's SMART scopes and organization_reference claim.

  Body fields are optional: `client_id` overrides the custodian's default
  (iss+sub+client_id) and `scope` ({{scope}}) requests specific SMART scopes —
  omit either to fall back to the custodian's configured defaults. You can also
  pass RFC 9396 `authorization_details`. The AS still decides what it grants.
}
